 "deepDive": {
  "domain": "Kraków deep dive + Culture, media & sport",
  "title": "Kraków, a week after the Rynek attack",
  "body": "A week after the Nov 7 attack that killed six people, Kraków is reopening its Main Square bit by bit. Horse-drawn carriages, whose drivers and horses were among those hurt, went back on Saturday. The same day, St Mary's Basilica held two free memorial concerts. Nine of the injured are still in hospital.\n\n**Security is the main question.** Mayor Gibała has asked the government for an urgent 10m zł to equip a new City Security and Monitoring Centre, which would put police, fire, crisis and city-guard dispatchers in one room. Kraków MPs from several parties back the request. The city already plans a 6m zł scheme of bollards and number-plate cameras for the Main and Small Market squares. The Akcja dla Krakowa group wants it extended to every Old Town entry. Nationally, the Interior Ministry is reviewing gun law, and prosecutors are still looking into motive and possible accomplices.\n\n**Ordinary city business is coming back.** The deputy mayor announced a 'new opening' for the overdue General Plan. Exemption renewals for the Clean Transport Zone start on Tuesday. The council's Nov 18 session will vote on fares and on suspending the Lviv partnership. The abrupt end of the Wisła stadium commercialisation contracts is another early test of how Gibała's team handles contracts it inherited.\n\n*Analysis:* For a mayor who took office promising to cut costs, the attack changes the arithmetic. Security spending is now unavoidable, just as he has to present a first budget for a city with debt close to 90% of revenue.",
